<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Modu Chanyu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/209-bce">209</a>–174 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Modu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Modu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanyu">Chanyu</a> was ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> from 209 to 174 BCE and founder of the Xiongnu Empire as a unified steppe power. He defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donghu">Donghu</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a> and inflicted a heavy defeat on the Han founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a> at Baideng in 200 BCE. This forced the Han to adopt the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heqin">heqin</a> policy of tribute and marriage alliances.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>According to the account of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Shiji</a>, Modu was a gifted child whose father Touman wanted the son of another wife to succeed him. Touman sent Modu to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a> as a hostage and then attacked them, hoping they would kill him, but Modu stole a fast horse and escaped, and was made commander of 10,000 horsemen.</p><p>He trained his followers with a whistling arrow, having them shoot at whatever it flew toward, and tested their loyalty by ordering them to shoot his favourite horse and then a favourite wife, executing those who hesitated. In 209 BCE he had them shoot his father on a hunting trip and proclaimed himself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanyu">chanyu</a>. He then executed his rival half-brother, his stepmother and officials who refused to support him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> invasion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ordos-plateau">Ordos Plateau</a> in 215 BCE had come as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> confederation faced a leadership crisis, and Modu used the resulting militarisation to build a centralised political structure. By 208 BCE he had defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donghu">Donghu</a>, whose remnants split into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> and Wuhuan, and he went on to subdue the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingling">Dingling</a> and defeat the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a> in 203 BCE. His empire reached from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-river">Liao River</a> in the east to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamir-mountains">Pamir Mountains</a> in the west and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-baikal">Lake Baikal</a> in the north.</p><p>In 200 BCE Emperor Gaozu of Han led an army north and advanced to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/datong">Pingcheng</a> with 40,000 men, where Modu surrounded him with 40,000 cavalry. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> withdrew some of their men, and Gaozu broke out. He then made peace through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heqin">heqin</a>, a marriage alliance with gifts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, wine and food. In 192 BCE Modu proposed marriage to Empress Dowager Lü Zhi, in a letter meant to intimidate and mock her. She refused humbly, and kept up the heqin policy and tribute.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Modu died in 174 BCE and was succeeded by his son Laoshang. Historians note that Han weakness gave Modu and his successors a steady flow of luxury and staple tribute to pass down to the aristocracy that supported them. Christopher Beckwith has pointed out that the story of his youth resembles a widespread class of folk tales of an abandoned young hero who proves his worth and becomes king. In Turkic languages Modu is known as Mete Khan, and the Turkish Land Forces take 209 BCE as their symbolic founding date.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Modu Chanyu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Southern Xiongnu grew out of the Xiongnu Empire founded by <b>Modu Chanyu</b> after 209 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/southern-xiongnu">Southern Xiongnu · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Xianbei emerged from the ruin of the Donghu, the 'Eastern Hu', a nomadic confederation of the eastern steppe that <b>Modu</b>, chanyu of the Xiongnu, destroyed around 206 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/xianbei">Xianbei · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It was unified and organised by <b>Modu Chanyu</b> (r. 209–174 BCE), who seized power from his father Touman in a coup described by the historian Sima Qian.</blockquote><a href="/empire/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Xiongnu ruler Touman even sent his son <b>Modu</b> to them as a hostage, a sign of Yuezhi superiority at the time.</blockquote><a href="/empire/yuezhi">Yuezhi · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 215 BCE the Qin general Meng Tian drove the Xiongnu ruler Touman from the Ordos Loop of the Yellow River, and in 209 BCE Touman’s son <b>Modu Chanyu</b> took power.</blockquote><a href="/government/nomadic-confederation">Nomadic confederation · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the Records of the Grand Historian, the Donghu demanded <b>Modu's</b> best horse, then one of his wives, then land; Modu granted the first two and answered the third by destroying them.</blockquote><a href="/state/donghu">Donghu</a></li></ul></section>
